WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.085
Episode nine: the job service from the terminal.

2
00:00:03.085 --> 00:00:06.492
This video assumes APEX installed, as in episode one.

3
00:00:06.492 --> 00:00:16.969
Run these commands from the APEX folder: uv run runs them in APEX's environment, as the README's installation sets it up.

4
00:00:16.969 --> 00:00:23.913
A root is a directory that holds the job service's state: its runs, its jobs and its tokens.

5
00:00:23.913 --> 00:00:28.820
Each root has at most one job service, running in the background.

6
00:00:28.820 --> 00:00:30.858
Every command takes --root.

7
00:00:30.858 --> 00:00:39.824
If you leave it out, APEX uses $APEX_HOME, and if that is not set, ~/.apex/service.

8
00:00:39.824 --> 00:00:50.219
This starts the job service of the root in the background.

9
00:00:50.219 --> 00:01:04.623
This shows whether the service is running and lists the jobs by state.

10
00:01:04.623 --> 00:01:06.773
The REST API uses a bearer token.

11
00:01:06.773 --> 00:01:11.919
The token is created the first time you ask for it and can be read by you only.

12
00:01:11.919 --> 00:01:17.877
This video does not print one: tokens are secrets.

13
00:01:17.877 --> 00:01:23.627
To serve the REST API as well, give a host and port, as in this command.

14
00:01:23.627 --> 00:01:25.305
We only type it here.

15
00:01:25.305 --> 00:01:38.946
Keep the host at 127.0.0.1 unless a TLS proxy is in front of the service.

16
00:01:38.946 --> 00:01:40.591
This stops the job service.

17
00:01:40.591 --> 00:01:52.979
Jobs that are already running go on, and their workers record their end themselves.

18
00:01:52.979 --> 00:02:04.813
We check again: the service is no longer running.

19
00:02:04.813 --> 00:02:11.981
The stop command can also wait longer, with --timeout: Seconds to wait for the service to end (default 30).

20
00:02:11.981 --> 00:02:15.398
The exit code is 2 if it is still stopping by then.

21
00:02:15.398 --> 00:02:23.725
We only type it.

22
00:02:23.725 --> 00:02:27.023
This rebuilds the run catalogue from the run directories.

23
00:02:27.023 --> 00:02:38.110
Use it if the catalogue no longer matches the run directories.

24
00:02:38.110 --> 00:02:45.210
If you use several roots, pass the same --root to every command.

25
00:02:45.210 --> 00:02:47.045
Expecting stop to end jobs.

26
00:02:47.045 --> 00:02:48.676
Running jobs keep going.

27
00:02:48.676 --> 00:02:53.483
Their workers record their end.

28
00:02:53.483 --> 00:03:00.302
So: start, status and stop act on one service per root, and stopping it leaves running jobs alone.

29
00:03:00.302 --> 00:03:06.133
Next, in episode ten: approving a submission.

30
00:03:06.133 --> 00:03:11.633
Thank you for watching.
